              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "If you are looking for free satellite radio, the options depend on whether you want to listen to **SiriusXM** (the major commercial satellite provider) without paying, find **free digital alternatives** that mimic the satellite radio experience, or set up **Free-to-Air (FTA) satellite hardware** to pull in free global broadcasts. \n\n---\n\n### Option 1: Listening to SiriusXM for Free\nSiriusXM is a subscription-based service, but there are several official ways to listen for free:\n\n* **The SiriusXM \"Free Access\" Plan (Ad-Supported):** SiriusXM offers a completely free, ad-supported tier for select vehicles in the United States. \n  * **How it works:** It requires no credit card and can be activated directly from your vehicle\u2019s dashboard. It features dozens of ad-supported music, sports, talk, and news channels (like SiriusXM Hits 1, 80s on 8, and Pop2K).\n  * **The Catches:** It is only available in vehicles equipped with newer **360L hybrid radios** (often found in newer models of Chevy, GMC, Ford, Dodge, Subaru, Volvo, Cadillac, and Nissan). It only works in your car (no mobile app streaming), and **you must listen at least once every 60 days** or the free tier will be permanently deactivated for your radio.\n* **Standard 3-Month In-Car Trials:** If you have an inactive SiriusXM radio in your car, you can frequently sign up for a **3-month free trial** on the SiriusXM website. For vehicle trials, they usually do not require you to input a credit card, meaning you won\u2019t be auto-billed when it ends. \n* **Partner Streaming Promotions:** If you want to listen on your phone or smart speakers instead of a car, look for partner promotions:\n  * **Samsung Galaxy Store:** Frequently offers a **6-month free trial** of SiriusXM streaming for new subscribers.\n  * **T-Mobile:** Often offers a **6-month \"On Us\"** streaming trial for active postpaid wireless subscribers.\n* **\"Listen Free\" Broadcast Events:** Twice a year (typically for two weeks around late May/early June and again around Thanksgiving), SiriusXM hosts \"Listen Free\" events. During these periods, they temporarily unlock inactive vehicle satellite radios, allowing anyone to listen to dozens of premium channels without signing up or doing anything.\n\n---\n\n### Option 2: Free Digital Alternatives (No Satellite Needed)\nIf you like the \"curated, DJ-hosted, genre-specific\" style of satellite radio but want a completely free app experience on your phone or in-car dashboard (using Android Auto or Apple CarPlay), try these:\n\n* **Dash Radio:** A completely free, 24/7 digital radio platform with over 80 stations curated by real DJs and musicians. It mimics the feel of SiriusXM closely but is entirely free and doesn't rely on traditional ad-breaks.\n* **iHeartRadio / TuneIn:** Excellent for streaming live terrestrial FM/AM radio stations from all over the country, along with custom digital-only genre channels, sports talk, and podcasts. \n* **Pandora:** Owned by SiriusXM, Pandora offers a free, ad-supported tier where you can build customized radio stations based on your favorite artists.\n\n---\n\n### Option 3: True Free-to-Air (FTA) Satellite Radio\nIf you are a hardware enthusiast who wants to pull in *actual* free satellite radio signals from space using physical equipment:\n\n* **What you need:** You will need a Free-to-Air (FTA) satellite dish (C-band or Ku-band) and an FTA satellite receiver box. \n* **What you can get:** Unlike encrypted pay-TV satellite networks, there are hundreds of satellites in orbit broadcasting unencrypted, completely legal \"Free-to-Air\" TV and radio signals. \n* **Where to point it:** In North America, pointing a Ku-band dish at the **Galaxy 19 satellite (97\u00b0 West)** will give you access to over 100 free TV and radio stations from around the world. You can receive international news, music, and culture broadcasts completely free of charge, with no monthly subscription.",
